    "lawCode": "Amd §§532 & 532-a, Pub Auth L",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S3624\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the public authorities law, in relation to granting\nthe New York State Bridge Authority the power to issue bonds and notes\nin an aggregate principal amount not to exceed $153,255,000\n\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThis bill would enable the New York State Bridge Authority\n(\"Authority\"), to issue bonds in an outstanding aggregate principal\namount not to exceed $ 153,255,000.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSections 1 and 3 of the bill would amend Public Authorities Law\n(\"PAL\") §§ 532 and 532-a to change the authorization to issue the\nAuthority's bonds or notes to allow the Authority to issue bonds in\nthe outstanding aggregate principal amount of $ 153,255,000.\n\nSection 2 of the bill would amend PAL § 532 to provide that the\nAuthority could refund its bonds in an amount greater than\n$153,255,000 if the Authority would realize a savings to its debt\nservice payments by doing so.\n\nSection 4 of the bill provides for an immediate effective date.\n\n EXISTING LAW :\n\nThe limitation on the authorized aggregate principal amount of bonds\nor notes was last extended in 1993 (Chapter 584 of the Laws of 1993).\n\nPAL §§ 532 and 532-a limit the Authority's bonding authorization to an\naggregate of $100,000,000.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nThe Assembly passed a similar bill, A.1153, in 2008.\n\n STATEMENT IN SUPPORT :\n\nThe Authority's five Hudson River crossings, with a combined current\nreplacement cost of $1,223,803,000, are critical links in the\nnortheast regional transportation system and the Mid Hudson region,\nwith more than 58,000,000 crossings annually.\n\nThe Authority has outstanding debt in the amount of $53,255,000 as of\nFebruary 1, 2009.\n\nThe Authority last issued bonds for its capital maintenance projects\nin 2002 in the principal amount of $50,000,000. The Authority has only\n$ 16,478,444 remaining under its present debt cap, excluding certain\nrefunding obligations. The Proposed amendment would allow the\nAuthority to issue obligations under a total outstanding debt cap of\n$153,255,000, including its currently outstanding debt.\n\nThe Authority needs additional bonding power to provide for its\nregular capital maintenance program. The Authority's long-term capital\nprogram contemplates the issuance of debt and the use of pay-as-you go\nfinancing from toll revenues. The expanded note and bond borrowing\ncapacity would also allow greater flexibility for the Authority to\nrespond to potential emergency needs, and to finance project\nexpenditures in advance of receipt of other available moneys.\n\n BUDGET IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone to the State. The Authority will incur debt service payments\nuntil the maturity date of any bonds or notes issued. The Authority's\nbonds are retired from its tolls and other revenues.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
